; A `build` BUFFER IS ZERO-FILLED, and nothing said so until now.
; expect: 0 0
; inputs: 0 1
;
; Two inputs, not the default nine: `run` ignores its argument, and reduction
; inlines it at every call site, so nine calls is nine 16-element tables built
; in one procedure -- which on x86 is more spilled operands than there are
; scratch registers. That ceiling belongs to the backend and provoking it from
; a conformance case would measure the harness.
;
; Four hosts agree by accident of four different mechanisms: Go's `make`, the
; JVM's `new long[n]`, `new Array(n).fill(0)` on JavaScript, and VirtualAlloc
; handing back committed pages on windows. Agreement across four targets with no
; specification and no test is exactly the shape of `split-words`, which passed
; every check for two months while returning different answers per target.
;
; It became load-bearing when examples/json/tree.oro made 0 the `none` sentinel
; for a node's `kid` and `sib`: every link that is never written is a
; correctness claim about what a fresh buffer contains.
;
; This reads every slot of a buffer that has had exactly ONE element written,
; and sums the rest. A host that leaves a buffer uninitialised, or sparse, or
; filled with `undefined`, cannot answer 0.
(def run (fn (n)
  (let (build 16 (fn (b) (set b 0 7)))
    (fn (t)
      (loop ((acc 0) (i 1))
        (@ge i 16)  acc
        else        (again (@add acc (t i)) (@add i 1)))))))
